---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO, url } from '../lib/catalog';
---
<Base title="Contribute" description="Add a product, correct a fiche, or record a verified review — everything happens through a pull request on GitHub.">
  <section class="wrap-narrow section prose">
    <h1>Contribute</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      No account to create here, because there is no account system. Everything is a file in a
      public repository, and every change is a pull request anyone can read.
    </p>

    <h2>Add a product</h2>
    <p>
      Copy <span class="mono">data/tools/_template.yaml</span>, fill it in, name it after the
      slug, open a pull request. The validator runs on the PR and tells you exactly what is
      missing.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/flowmetrik/proptech-atlas
cd proptech-atlas && npm install
cp data/tools/_template.yaml data/tools/my-product.yaml
$EDITOR data/tools/my-product.yaml
npm run data:validate`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Correct a fiche</h2>
    <p>
      Every product page links to its own YAML file and to a one-click edit form. If something
      is wrong — a feature that no longer exists, a vendor that got acquired, a market we got
      wrong — change the line and explain the change in the PR description.
    </p>

    <h2>Record a verified review</h2>
    <p>
      This is the contribution with the strictest rule. You are not writing a testimonial: you
      are reporting an aggregate that is publicly visible today, with the URL and the date you
      read it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`reviews:
  - source: G2
    url: https://www.g2.com/products/example/reviews
    rating: 4.4
    scale: 5
    count: 812
    sampled_on: 2026-08-25`}</code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>A pull request that adds a rating without a source URL and a sampling date will be
      rejected.</strong> Not as a formality — an unsourced number is indistinguishable from an
      invented one, and the whole point of this dataset is that a machine can trust it.
    </div>

    <h2>What makes a good fiche</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Write the <span class="mono">real_estate_use</span> field like you are briefing a colleague.</strong> Concrete situation, real workflow. This is the field the project exists for.</li>
      <li><strong>Features are nouns, not adjectives.</strong> "Trust accounting with per-property ledgers", not "powerful and intuitive".</li>
      <li><strong>Do not copy the vendor's homepage.</strong> Paraphrase, and say what the product is rather than what it promises.</li>
      <li><strong>One primary category.</strong> If it genuinely spans more, use <span class="mono">also_in</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Leave a field out rather than guessing.</strong> Especially prices, which move.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you are a vendor</h2>
    <p>
      You are welcome to submit and to correct your own entry — say so in the pull request.
      What you cannot do is remove a competitor, add a rating, or turn the description into
      marketing copy. If you think an entry is unfair, open an issue: it gets marked
      <span class="mono">disputed</span> and looked at in public.
    </p>

    <p style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <a class="btn" href={`${REPO}/issues/new/choose`} rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`} rel="noopener">Full contributing guide</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={url('api')}>API reference</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
